---
title: "The allegory — FrontierJS"
description: "A software system is a town, and the app is its biggest building. Running it means the roads, the utilities, the watch, the inspector and the records office too."
render: static
---

<!--
  The same arc as the-story, told as a town. The tools named here are the ones
  that page names; a new one is checked there first.
-->
<section id="top" class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The allegory</p>
    <h1>Your app is one building in a town.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Building software is one job. Running it is a dozen: the data, the
      services, the servers, the secrets, the deploys, the backups, the logs at
      two in the morning. Picture all of it as a town and the work gets easier
      to see. The app is the biggest building on main street. Someone still
      has to run the rest of the place.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="plan-wrap" aria-labelledby="plan-title">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 id="plan-title" class="sr-only">The town plan</h2>
    <nav class="plan" aria-label="Districts">
      <a class="lot app" href="#ch-03">
        <span class="num">03</span>
        <strong>Main street</strong>
        <span class="floors">
          <span>UI</span>
          <span>API</span>
          <span>Data</span>
        </span>
      </a>
      <a class="lot post" href="#ch-04"><span class="num">04</span><strong>Post office</strong><span>roads out of town</span></a>
      <a class="lot survey" href="#ch-01"><span class="num">01</span><strong>Surveyor</strong><span>the map</span></a>
      <a class="lot night" href="#ch-05"><span class="num">05</span><strong>Night crew</strong><span>jobs, schedules, agents</span></a>
      <a class="lot planning" href="#ch-02"><span class="num">02</span><strong>Planning office</strong><span>decided once</span></a>
      <a class="lot utilities" href="#ch-06"><span class="num">06</span><strong>Utilities</strong><span>servers, config, secrets, ports</span></a>
      <a class="lot watch" href="#ch-07"><span class="num">07</span><strong>Watchtower</strong><span>traces, health</span></a>
      <a class="lot inspector" href="#ch-08"><span class="num">08</span><strong>Inspector</strong><span>tests, proofs</span></a>
      <a class="lot records" href="#ch-12"><span class="num">12</span><strong>Records office</strong><span>why it's like this</span></a>
      <a class="lot hall" href="#ch-10"><span class="num">10</span><strong>Town hall</strong><span>you, and sometimes an agent beside you</span></a>
      <a class="lot depot" href="#ch-09"><span class="num">09</span><strong>Depot</strong><span>ship, roll back, restore</span></a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapters">
  <div class="container">
    <ol class="arc">
      <li id="ch-00">
        <span class="n">00</span>
        <div>
          <h2>A town, not a building</h2>
          <p>Somebody writes the code. Somebody also keeps the database healthy, lets the payment provider in, rotates the keys, watches the logs, ships the fix and checks there's a backup from before it. In a big company those are departments. In a small team they're the same three people. On your own, they're all you.</p>
          <p>None of those jobs is hard on its own. What wears you down is that each arrives with its own tool, its own config file and its own words, and none of them knows the others exist. It's like running a town where the water board and the road crew have never met.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">The hard part isn't any one job. It's keeping them in agreement.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-01">
        <span class="n">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">The surveyor</p>
          <h2>Walk the land</h2>
          <p>Nobody runs a town without a map. What's built, and where? Which streets lead where? Who holds the keys to which building, and what stops working if the bridge closes?</p>
          <p>In most projects that map lives in someone's head. In FrontierJS it starts on the ground. The data is described once, in <code>db/schema.lite</code>, and every part of the app is a folder you can see from the root: <code>api/</code>, <code>web/</code>, <code>site/</code>, and whatever you build next beside them. List the directory and you've read the street plan.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">You can't look after what you can't see.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-02">
        <span class="n">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">The planning office</p>
          <h2>Settle it once</h2>
          <p>A good town doesn't argue about road widths every time someone builds a house. It decided once, wrote it down, and every new street follows. That's what makes a town easy to walk: you can turn a corner you've never turned and still know roughly what's there.</p>
          <p>FrontierJS makes those decisions up front and keeps them. A model is singular and capitalized, so <code>model Lead</code> is <code>db.lead</code> everywhere. The app has three nouns: Model, Service, Resource. Ports come from a formula, not a list. Who may read or change a record is settled on the model, and nowhere else.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">A decision made once can't be made two different ways.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-03">
        <span class="n">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">Main street</p>
          <h2>Raise the buildings</h2>
          <p>The app is the biggest building in town, the one people come to visit. It has floors: data at the bottom, the API in the middle, the screens on top, and a front desk that checks who you are. Most frameworks are about this building, and fair enough. It's where most of the work happens.</p>
          <p>But it stands on a street, draws from the mains and gets its post from the post office. In FrontierJS its floors are Model, Service and Resource, all raised from the same schema. The locks are on the model itself: a <code>@@gate</code> on Lead holds whether someone came in through the front door, a background job or an agent.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">The app is the main building. It's still one building.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/flow/">Flow</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-04">
        <span class="n">04</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">The post office</p>
          <h2>Roads out of town</h2>
          <p>No town feeds itself. Mail goes out, deliveries come in, payments cross the border, and the neighbors want to hear when something changes. Every road out is somewhere a parcel can go missing.</p>
          <p>Each outside service gets one address. They're declared in one place, each with its own timeouts and retries, and every call goes through <code>app.conduit.send()</code>. When something changes inside, webhooks go out at least once, and try again when nobody answers.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Every road out of town is on the map.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/seams/">Seams</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-05">
        <span class="n">05</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">The night crew</p>
          <h2>Work done while you sleep</h2>
          <p>Much of what keeps a town going happens after dark. Streets get swept, lamps get lit, the post is sorted before dawn. Nobody asks for it each night. It just happens, and it follows the same rules as everything else.</p>
          <p>Caravan runs the queue and the schedule, on SQLite. A follow-up can be queued in the same transaction as the save that prompted it, so it only runs if the save did. A notification lands in the app and in an inbox at once. And an agent working through MCP joins the night crew carrying exactly the keys your gates already hand out.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Work nobody clicked answers to the same rules.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-06">
        <span class="n">06</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">Utilities</p>
          <h2>Water, power and land</h2>
          <p>You only notice the utilities when they stop. Every building needs ground to stand on, power, water and a locked meter room. Software needs servers, storage, config for each environment, secrets that never belong in the repo, and addresses that never collide.</p>
          <p>Config is read in one place, through <code>$.config</code>. <code>fli env:*</code> moves environment values between machines. Ports follow a formula of environment, category, project and service, so dev, test and prod never share a door. Outpost runs on each server and acts only on signed instructions.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Where the software lives is part of the plan.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-07">
        <span class="n">07</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">The watchtower</p>
          <h2>Keep watch</h2>
          <p>A town needs someone on the wall. Not to fix things, but to notice them: smoke over the east side, a bridge that's gone quieter than it should, a light that went out at the wrong hour.</p>
          <p>Every request carries a trace id. Health checks and metrics are registered next to the code they describe. The devtools panel shows calls as they happen, and Basecamp, itself a FrontierJS app, watches the whole fleet.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Hear about it before a resident has to tell you.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-08">
        <span class="n">08</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">The inspector</p>
          <h2>Inspect before anyone moves in</h2>
          <p>Nobody moves into a building the inspector hasn't seen. Change in a town passes a few checkpoints on purpose, not to slow it down, but so that what opens is what was meant.</p>
          <p><code>createTestEnv</code> gives a test a real database and a real API, built from the schema. Migrations come from diffing it. <code>fli test:access</code> writes the whole access policy out as a snapshot, so a change to who can do what shows up in review. <code>fli proves</code> reads a change and names the checks it needs, and <code>fli done</code> says whether you're finished.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Know what a change does before anyone lives with it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-09">
        <span class="n">09</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">The depot</p>
          <h2>Open the doors, keep a fire brigade</h2>
          <p>Opening day isn't the end of the job. It's where looking after the place begins. Something will break, and then what matters is how quickly the old version goes back up and whether there's a copy of what was lost.</p>
          <p><code>fli release:mint</code> works out exactly what would ship, without changing anything. <code>fli deploy</code> ships it, the deploy journal records every attempt, and <code>fli deploy:rollback</code> puts the last release back. <code>fli db:backup</code> takes a hot backup of every database the schema declares. One gap remains: restoring a backup into production is still done by hand.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Production is where the responsibility starts.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-10">
        <span class="n">10</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">Town hall</p>
          <h2>One desk for the whole place</h2>
          <p>Somewhere there's a desk where everything lands: the reports, the requests, the alarms, the plans. In a small town it's one person's desk, and whoever sits there needs to see the whole place and act on it without crossing town for every errand.</p>
          <p><code>fli</code> is that desk. Its commands are written in markdown, and your project's own sit beside the framework's. <code>fli check</code> turns your conventions into checks that fail, so nobody has to remember them. Basecamp shows the fleet. And when an agent lends a hand, it reads the same map and keeps the same rules you do.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Less running around for the person who answers for all of it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-11" class="reveal">
        <span class="n">11</span>
        <div>
          <h2>One set of plans</h2>
          <p>What makes a town feel like a town, and not a pile of buildings, is that everyone works from the same plans. The road crew and the water board use the same street names. The inspector checks against the code the planners wrote. Nobody keeps a private map.</p>
          <p>Take the <code>@@gate</code> on Lead. You wrote it once, and it turns up all over town:</p>
          <table class="views">
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Main street</th><td>the query refuses rows the caller can't see, and the screen hides the button</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Front desk</th><td>a refused call gets its status from the one place that maps errors</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Night crew</th><td>an agent isn't offered a tool it isn't allowed to use</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Inspector</th><td><code>fli test:access</code> puts any change to it in the diff</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>Laravel showed how calm an app becomes when its decisions are made once and kept consistent. FrontierJS carries that past the front door: out to the utilities, the watchtower, the depot and the records office.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">One set of plans for the whole town.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li id="ch-12">
        <span class="n">12</span>
        <div>
          <p class="district">The records office</p>
          <h2>Keep the records</h2>
          <p>Towns outlast the people who built them. There's usually a reason a road bends where it does, and the town that wrote it down doesn't knock out a wall it still needs.</p>
          <p>Code says what. It rarely says why. In a FrontierJS project the reasons live in <code>DECISIONS.md</code>, what's broken in <code>ISSUES.md</code>, and what isn't built yet in <code>IDEAS/</code>. <code>fli</code> reads all three: <code>fli next</code> suggests what to work on, <code>fli file</code> logs a problem, and <code>fli decide</code> records a decision.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">The next person to read the plans might be you, a year from now.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="closing">
      <p>FrontierJS is a framework for building and running software systems. The app is where it starts. The town is what it's for.</p>
      <p class="see">The same arc, with the tools up front: <a href="/the-story/">The story</a> · <a href="/map/">Map</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero { padding: 4.5rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .eyebrow { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); letter-spacing: -.03em; margin: .3em 0; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.12rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 60ch; line-height: 1.6; }
  .sr-only { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; }

  /* The plan: the grid's own background shows through the gaps as the streets. */
  .plan-wrap { padding: 1rem 0 2rem; }
  .plan {
    display: grid; gap: .7rem; padding: .7rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    grid-template-areas:
      "app app post survey"
      "app app night planning"
      "utilities utilities watch inspector"
      "records hall hall depot";
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    border: 1px dashed var(--rule); border-radius: 10px;
    font-family: var(--font-primary);
  }
  .lot {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .15rem;
    padding: .7rem .8rem; min-height: 5.5rem;
    background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 4px;
    text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink-mute); font-size: .8rem; line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .lot:hover, .lot:focus-visible { border-color: var(--color-primary); }
  .lot strong { color: var(--ink); font-size: .95rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .lot .num { font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .14em; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .app { grid-area: app; border-color: var(--color-primary); }
  .post { grid-area: post; }
  .survey { grid-area: survey; }
  .night { grid-area: night; }
  .planning { grid-area: planning; }
  .utilities { grid-area: utilities; }
  .watch { grid-area: watch; }
  .inspector { grid-area: inspector; }
  .records { grid-area: records; }
  .hall { grid-area: hall; }
  .depot { grid-area: depot; }

  .floors { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .3rem; margin-top: auto; padding-top: .6rem; }
  .floors span { padding: .4rem .6rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 3px; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .plan {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      grid-template-areas:
        "app app"
        "survey planning"
        "post night"
        "utilities utilities"
        "watch inspector"
        "depot records"
        "hall hall";
    }
    .floors { flex-direction: row; }
    .floors span { flex: 1; text-align: center; }
  }

  .chapters { padding: 1rem 0 5rem; }
  .arc { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .arc li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; padding: 1.6rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--sticky-offset) + 1rem); }
  .arc .n { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .14em; color: var(--ink-mute); padding-top: .3rem; }
  .arc h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  .arc p { margin: 0; max-width: 64ch; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .arc p + p, .arc table + p, .arc p + table { margin-top: .45rem; }
  .arc .district { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: .2rem; }
  .arc .district + h2 { margin-top: 0; }
  .takeaway { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .see { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .reveal h2 { color: var(--color-primary); }

  .views { border-collapse: collapse; max-width: 64ch; width: 100%; font-size: .92rem; }
  .views th { text-align: left; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); padding: .35rem 1rem .35rem 0; white-space: nowrap; vertical-align: top; }
  .views td { color: var(--ink-soft); padding: .35rem 0; }
  .views tr + tr > * { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }

  .closing { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 2rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
  .closing p:first-child { font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.5; max-width: 52ch; color: var(--ink); font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
</style>
